[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r6
diagnosis_text:
The failure is in the routing subsystem on r6: FRRouting (zebra/ospfd) is not running after the upgrade attempt. Evidence: on r6, systemctl shows “frr.service Active: inactive (dead)”, and vtysh reports “ospfd is not running.” r6’s FRR running-config is essentially empty (no router ospf), and its RIB/kernel table contains only connected routes (46/8, 56/8, 60/8, 67/8), with no learned OSPF routes. Upstream, r1 has no routes to 67.0.0.0/8, 70.0.0.0/8, or 17.0.0.0/8 (“% Network not in table”), and r4/r5 OSPF neighbor lists show no adjacency to r6 on S (r4-eth2 ↔ r6-eth0) or V (r5-eth1 ↔ r6-eth1), confirming the OSPF break at r6. Interfaces on r6 are up, so the failure is control plane, not data plane. Mechanism: r6 is the ABR/relay between the backbone and the r7 area; with ospfd stopped on r6, no LSAs/summary routes for the r7 region can pass into the backbone, isolating 67/8, 70/8, and 17.x.x.x from r1 and the rest of the network. Localization: r6, OSPF process and adjacencies on eth0 (to r4) and eth1 (to r5). Root cause: the FRR upgrade on r6 left the FRR service stopped (and configuration not loaded), dropping OSPF adjacencies. Corrective fix: restart and enable FRR on r6 (systemctl start frr; ensure ospfd=yes in /etc/frr/daemons and proper OSPF config present), then verify OSPF neighbors on S/V and that r1 receives the r7 routes. === END ===